/**
  @page guides-getting-started-first-steps First steps with Rodin
  @brief Build a small program and identify the objects used in a finite element solve.

  This guide starts with an installed Rodin library. A small program constructs
  a triangular mesh and a scalar finite element space, then prints their sizes.
  No equation is solved yet: the goal is to establish the objects on which a
  variational problem will be built.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-getting-started-first-steps-structure Create an application

  A minimal application needs a source file, `main.cpp`, and a `CMakeLists.txt`.
  Mesh data can be generated by the program or loaded from a file. This example
  generates its mesh and therefore needs no external data.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-first-steps-cmake Configure the build

  The imported CMake targets provide Rodin's include directories and link
  dependencies. The application requests C++20 explicitly.

  @code{.cmake}
  cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.16)
  project(FirstRodinProgram LANGUAGES CXX)

  find_package(Rodin REQUIRED)

  add_executable(first_rodin main.cpp)
  target_compile_features(first_rodin PRIVATE cxx_std_20)
  target_link_libraries(first_rodin PRIVATE
    Rodin::Geometry
    Rodin::Variational
  )
  @endcode

  If Rodin is installed outside the standard search paths, pass its installation
  prefix through `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` when configuring the application. The
  @ref guides-getting-started-installation "installation guide" explains the setup.

  @section guides-getting-started-first-steps-hello Construct a mesh and a space

  The mesh describes the domain; `P1` describes scalar functions that are
  continuous across triangles and affine within each triangle.

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <iostream>
  #include <Rodin/Geometry.h>
  #include <Rodin/Variational.h>

  using namespace Rodin;
  using namespace Rodin::Geometry;
  using namespace Rodin::Variational;

  int main()
  {
    Mesh mesh = Mesh().UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {8, 8});
    mesh.scale(1.0 / 7.0);
    P1 vh(mesh);

    std::cout << "Vertices: " << mesh.getVertexCount() << '\n';
    std::cout << "Triangles: " << mesh.getCellCount() << '\n';
    std::cout << "Dimension: " << mesh.getDimension() << '\n';
    std::cout << "P1 degrees of freedom: " << vh.getSize() << '\n';
    return 0;
  }
  @endcode

  `UniformGrid` takes vertex counts, rather than subdivision counts. Eight
  vertices per direction give seven intervals, so scaling by @f$1/7@f$ places
  the mesh on the unit square. There are @f$8^2=64@f$ vertices and
  @f$2(8-1)^2=98@f$ triangles. Scalar `P1` has one degree of freedom per vertex.

  @section guides-getting-started-first-steps-run Build and run

  Run these commands in the directory containing the two files:

  @code{.bash}
  cmake -S . -B build
  cmake --build build
  ./build/first_rodin
  @endcode

  The expected output is:

  @code{.text}
  Vertices: 64
  Triangles: 98
  Dimension: 2
  P1 degrees of freedom: 64
  @endcode

  @section guides-getting-started-first-steps-concepts From geometry to equations

  @subsection guides-getting-started-first-steps-mesh Meshes

  A mesh separates topological dimension from ambient dimension. A triangular
  surface embedded in three-dimensional space has topological dimension two
  and ambient dimension three. `getDimension()` and `getSpaceDimension()`
  expose these two quantities. See @ref guides-meshes "Meshes" for creation,
  loading, and traversal.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-first-steps-connectivity Connectivity

  Connectivity records incidence relations between geometric entities. For
  example, the Poisson tutorial computes edge-to-cell connectivity with
  `mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2)` to identify exterior edges in a
  two-dimensional mesh. See @ref guides-connectivity "Mesh connectivity".

  @subsection guides-getting-started-first-steps-fe Finite element fields

  A finite element space defines the approximation and the distribution of
  degrees of freedom. A `GridFunction` stores a particular field in that
  space. Scalar `P1` is the starting point here; other spaces have different
  continuity and numbering rules. See @ref fem-spaces "Finite element spaces".

  @subsection guides-getting-started-first-steps-functions Trial and test functions

  Trial and test functions are symbolic objects used to construct equations:

  @code{.cpp}
  TrialFunction u(vh);
  TestFunction v(vh);
  @endcode

  `u` represents the unknown field. `v` represents the test function used to
  form the discrete equations. Operators and integrals combine them into a
  variational problem. After a solve, `u.getSolution()` provides the computed
  grid function.

  @section guides-getting-started-first-steps-modules Finding the relevant API

  The <a href="modules.html">module reference</a> groups interfaces by their
  responsibilities: Geometry describes the domain, Variational describes
  fields and forms, Assembly constructs algebraic systems, Solver solves
  those systems, and IO exports the results. Optional models and integrations
  are listed there alongside their public headers.

  @section guides-getting-started-first-steps-next Continue with a solve

  Proceed to @ref guides-getting-started-first-problem "Your first problem"
  to build and run a Poisson solve. The @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson
  tutorial" explains the mathematical formulation and each stage of the code.
*/
